al coverage describes a dead heat heading into November. Kotek won their 2022 meeting by only a few points, and no Republican has won this office in more than four decades.\n\nRaters are split between Lean D and Likely D, and betting markets give Kotek roughly a three-in-four chance. Oregon's Democratic registration edge and the power of incumbency still favor Kotek, but her sub-50-percent polling standing keeps this far closer than the state's usual federal margins. Turnout around Portland and how the feisty first debate, with its clashes over Portland and national politics, lands with persuadable voters look likely to decide the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.91, "margin": 7.23}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7143, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6673, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6685, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7753, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7762, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7967, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7478, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.743, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7131, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.7358, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.7337, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.7182, "margin": 2.5}]}